    <exercise xml:id="quiz-for-loop-output">
      <title>Determine the Output</title>

      <task xml:id="quiz-for-loop-output-1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is printed?
          </p>
          <program language="matlab">
total = 0;
for n = 1:4
    total = total + n;
end
fprintf('total = %d\n', total)
          </program>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="quiz-for-loop-output-2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is printed?
          </p>
          <program language="matlab">
count = 0;
for n = 3:7
    if mod(n,2) == 1
        count = count + 1;
    end
end
fprintf('count = %d\n', count)
          </program>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="quiz-for-loop-output-3">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What does this function return when called with <pf>x = 3</pf>?
          </p>
          <program language="matlab">
function y = bump(x)
    y = x;
    for k = 1:5
        y = y + 2;
    end
end
          </program>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="quiz-for-loop-script-writing">
      <title>Write Scripts with for-Loops</title>

      <task xml:id="quiz-for-loop-script-writing-1">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Write a script that prints the integers from 1 through 8, one per line, using a <pf>for</pf>-loop and <pf>fprintf</pf>.
          </p>
          <program language="matlab">
% Write your script here



          </program>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="quiz-for-loop-script-writing-2">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Write a script that computes the sum of all multiples of 3 from 3 through 30. Store the result in <pf>total</pf>.
          </p>
          <program language="matlab">
% Write your script here



          </program>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="quiz-for-loop-construction-1">
        <title>Compute the Running Total</title>

        <introduction>
          <p>
            For each of the following, assume that:
            <ul>
              <li><pf>a</pf> is a random integer between 100 and 900, and</li>
              <li><pf>x</pf> is a random integer between 5 and 12</li>
            </ul>
            and write a <pf>for</pf>-loop that completes each task. 
          </p>
        </introduction>

        <task xml:id="quiz-for-loop-construction-1a">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute the sum of all integers between <pf>x</pf> and <pf>a</pf>. Store the result in <pf>total</pf>.
            </p>
            <program language="matlab">
              a = randi([100, 900]);
              x = randi([5, 12]);
              total = 0; % initialize
              % ==== Write your for loop here ====
              󠀠
              󠀠
              󠀠
              󠀠
              󠀠
            </program>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task xml:id="quiz-for-loop-construction-1b">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute the sum of all integers between <pf>0</pf> and <pf>a</pf> that are divisible by <pf>x</pf>. Store the result in <pf>total</pf>.
            </p>
            <program language="matlab">
              a = randi([100, 900]);
              x = randi([5, 12]);
              total = 0; % initialize
              % ==== Write your for loop here ====
              󠀠
              󠀠
              󠀠
              󠀠
              󠀠
            </program>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="quiz-for-loop-construction-2">
        <title>Approximating Pi with Leibniz's Formula</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            The Leibniz formula for <m>\pi</m> states that:
            <me>\frac{\pi}{4} = 1 - \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{5} - \frac{1}{7} + \frac{1}{9} - \cdots</me>
            This infinite series converges slowly to <m>\pi/4</m>, but we can approximate it by adding many terms. Work through the tasks below to approximate <m>\pi</m>.
          </p>

